WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:02.419
Take a look at this 
impossible figure.

00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:05.005
Much like the work of the artist 
M.C. Escher

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:08.049
this shows an object that seems 
to be three dimensional

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:10.385
but cannot actually exist 
off the screen.

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:13.221
On the other hand, this is 
a possible object.

00:00:13.388 --> 00:00:15.140
It could exist in real life.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.225
In 2001, researchers performed

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:19.394
a unique experiment with 
images like this.

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:22.522
They asked people with 
and without dyslexia

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.941
to identify which drawings 
were impossible.

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.319
The people with dyslexia proved 
significantly faster

00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:30.613
at recognizing the 
impossible figures.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.407
The researchers linked dyslexia

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:35.535
with a particular global 
visual spatial ability...

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:38.997
to process a whole image versus 
focusing on it part by part.

00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:42.834
This study was just one of several 
over the past few decades

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.211
that hinted at something 
many dyslexic people

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.921
have only anecdotally known.

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.632
Though they had difficulty with 
reading and writing...

00:00:49.924 --> 00:00:52.969
they repeatedly found enhanced 
cognitive strengths

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:54.095
in other areas.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:58.391
I have this kind of like 
model in my head.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:00.935
I can spin it around 
in my head

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:05.315
and see ways of deconstructing 
and flipping it around 

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:06.649
with a lot of these.

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:09.819
It’s the entire puzzle rather than 
just the piece of it.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.697
It brings another layer of thinking

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:14.783
and thinking outside the box 
and the blues.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.910
-Was it the blue...
-Blue sky thinking?

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.578
Blue sky thinking!

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:20.705
That doesn't make me 
superhuman

00:01:20.705 --> 00:01:23.333
it’s just like how my 
brain structure is.

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.877
Around 20% of the 
US population 

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:27.670
has been diagnosed 
with dyslexia

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.215
and their difficulties 
all trace back...

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:31.716
to here.

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:34.094
The dyslexic brain is 
structured differently

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:36.387
in a way that can create 
specific challenges...

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:39.182
but can also create 
specific advantages.

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:44.771
In terms of human evolution

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:47.065
the brain isn't naturally wired 
to read.

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:49.609
There's not one specific region 
for reading 

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:52.862
like there is for sleeping or 
engaging our fear response.

00:01:53.071 --> 00:01:55.657
Reading is an invention.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.119
It is invention that's only 
6000 years old.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.620
While there is so much

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.872
we still don't understand about 
the human brain...

00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:05.708
over the past few decades, 
a clearer picture

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:07.794
of how we read has emerged.

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:09.796
When we're young, 
we're activating

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.048
both hemispheres of 
our brains heavily...

00:02:12.173 --> 00:02:14.217
expending energy to learn 
how to read.

00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:16.761
But for fluent readers, 
the brain streamlines

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:19.556
more activation to structures 
in the left hemisphere...

00:02:19.722 --> 00:02:22.642
like this region known as 
the visual word form area...

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.936
which helps us recognize 
letters and words.

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:28.398
And this region, key to 
mapping letters to sounds

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:30.441
called phonological awareness.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:33.611
For example, breaking up the word cat 
into the sounds...

00:02:33.903 --> 00:02:35.989
cuh, ah, and tuh.

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:38.867
And there's Broca's area, 
which among other things

00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:42.328
helps with comprehension to understand 
what words actually mean.

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:45.665
Activated areas linked together 
with white matter.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.125
There are neural pathways

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:49.502
that turn into a complicated 
highway network

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:50.837
that allows fluent readers

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.173
to process a word 
within milliseconds.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.967
But in the dyslexic brain, 
these highways

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.595
and activated areas can look 
a lot different.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.723
Research using brain scans 
of dyslexic readers 

00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:03.474
showed that there was 
less activation

00:03:03.474 --> 00:03:05.476
in these areas of the 
left hemisphere...

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:09.230
suggesting there's often a pathway 
disruption in these systems

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.983
that help maps sounds to letters 
and decode words.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.068
But people with dyslexia

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:16.696
also consistently showed 
overactivation

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.698
in the brain's right hemisphere 
when reading.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.659
And that makes it 
more laborious.

00:03:22.076 --> 00:03:23.870
This could mean that while 
dyslexic people

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.122
may have trouble recognizing 
words and sounds...

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:28.708
they're working even harder 
to compensate elsewhere.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:31.377
The pathway disruptions can also come

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:34.380
with challenges in grammar, 
retrieving math facts

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.507
working memory, 
among other things too.

00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:39.260
Or often with dyslexia, 
neural pathways

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.596
are not disrupted, but just 
slowed down.

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:43.848
But I'm sort of 
mildly dyslexic.

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:46.226
My challenge is more with numbers.

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:47.393
Reading out loud.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:50.021
In class, you're stricken with panic.

00:03:50.021 --> 00:03:52.649
And then all the words 
start floating.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.484
You just you just can't 
read the words.

00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:57.654
Everyone else would be on to 
doing the exercise.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.657
And I’d be still trying to 
read the instructions.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:04.369
Exactly how people experience dyslexia 
varies greatly.

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:07.330
It has no bearing on 
an individual's intelligence...

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.290
but today it's commonly labeled 

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:12.502
a hereditary neurobiological 
learning disability.

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:15.672
Research has found explicit 
support and instruction

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.800
in areas like phonology, 
syntax, and spelling

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.594
can lead to success for 
dyslexic learners.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.681
But the reading brain is only 
part of the picture.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.643
Years of research has started 
corroborating the idea

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:31.396
that dyslexic people are 
prone to advantages

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:33.982
including high level reasoning, 
problem solving

00:04:33.982 --> 00:04:37.318
spatial processing, episodic memory, 
and creativity.

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:40.029
Because I have the full picture 
in my head

00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:42.115
sometimes I can make association

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.659
between things that they don't appear 
to be relevant.

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:47.870
We shake it up a bit if we kind of 
get people to think...

00:04:47.996 --> 00:04:50.665
you know, “Oh, I didn't 
think of that.”

00:04:50.748 --> 00:04:53.251
Being able to recall stories.

00:04:53.251 --> 00:04:55.295
You know, recall images.

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:56.587
I'll meet somebody 

00:04:56.587 --> 00:04:59.757
and I will stop them on 
the street and be like

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.343
“We know each other” and 
they will have no idea.

00:05:02.343 --> 00:05:06.639
I'm going to show you what 
a dyslexic high schooler did.

00:05:07.098 --> 00:05:08.433
I was floored.

00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:10.977
Gobsmacked, because he was drawing

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:13.354
from memory 
upside down.

00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:19.777
There are specific areas of strength 
in the right hemisphere...

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:23.114
that seem very much 
to be at play...

00:05:23.406 --> 00:05:25.700
with individuals 
with dyslexia.

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:29.120
While most functions do use 
both hemispheres of our brains...

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.998
the right hemisphere is typically 
associated with skills

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:34.292
like spatial abilities and 
visual imagery.

00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:37.295
In a study similar to the one 
with impossible figures...

00:05:37.587 --> 00:05:39.380
dyslexic people were 
better able

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.633
to identify letters in their periphery 
than other readers.

00:05:42.925 --> 00:05:44.677
Another study compared 
the abilities 

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:47.221
of college students with
and without dyslexia

00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:48.890
to memorize blurred images.

00:05:49.098 --> 00:05:52.060
Students with dyslexia significantly 
outperformed.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:55.897
And in 2022, researchers at
University of Strathclyde

00:05:55.897 --> 00:05:58.316
and University of Cambridge 
concluded that

00:05:58.316 --> 00:05:59.901
that many people 
with dyslexia

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:02.320
are specialized in exploring
the unknown.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:04.697
And they suggested that because of 
how prevalent

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:08.117
dyslexia is in the population, 
the form of cognition plays

00:06:08.117 --> 00:06:10.912
an essential role in enabling 
humans to adapt...

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:14.665
especially through collaboration 
between different kinds of people.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:17.335
These new ways of thinking 
about dyslexia

00:06:17.335 --> 00:06:20.546
are part of a wider movement 
to embrace neurodiversity...

00:06:20.963 --> 00:06:23.633
the idea that brains are simply 
wired differently

00:06:23.633 --> 00:06:27.387
and that these natural variations 
don't mean they're lacking.

00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:32.558
Stigma, misconceptions, and a core lack 
of understanding dyslexia

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.102
are still rampant.

00:06:34.102 --> 00:06:37.647
But the more we can learn about 
differently, organized brains

00:06:37.647 --> 00:06:40.608
the better we can teach, collaborate with, 
and empower them.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:44.362
And ultimately change the way 
we see dyslexia.

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:48.157
This constellation 
of strengths...

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:52.662
and weaknesses that go
hand in hand.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.081
You can imagine how much better

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:58.668
a child will feel when they know 
this is not a curse.

00:06:58.668 --> 00:07:00.294
This is not a disease.

00:07:00.294 --> 00:07:04.132
This is just a different organization 
in the brain.

